%T A136910 500050,500500,500505,505000,5000000,5000005,5000050,5000500,5000505,
%U A136910 5005000,5005050,5050000,5050005,50000000,50000005,50000050,50000500,50000505,50005000,50005005,50005050,50050000,50050500,50500000,50500005,50500050,500000000
%N A136910 Numbers k such that k and k^2 use only the digits 0, 2 and 5.
%C A136910 Generated with DrScheme.
%C A136910 From _M. F. Hasler_, Oct 12 2026: (Start)
%C A136910 Could be seen as irregular table where row n contains all n-digit terms.
%C A136910 All terms are multiples of 5, since their last nonzero digit must be a 5. The sequence a(n)/5 = 0, 1, 10, 100, 101, 1000, 1001, 1010, 10000, 10001, 10010, 10100, 100000, ... is not in the OEIS. It differs from A014417 starting at a(13)/5 = 10^5.
%C A136910 For any term a(n), a(n)*10 is also in the sequence, so we could focus on the sequence of "primitive" terms which are not multiple of 10. Divided by 5, they are 1, 101, 1001, 10001, 100001, 100101, 1000001, 1000101, ... (also not in the OEIS).
%C A136910 All terms must have their last 4 digits (i.e., a(n) mod 10^4) among {0, 5, 50, 500, 505, 5000, 5005, 5050}: For example, if a number ends in 25, then its square's hundreds digit is 6; if a number ends in 205, its square's 10 thousands digit is 4, 6 or 9. (End)
%H A136910 David A. Corneth, <a href="/A136910/b136910.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..12890</a> (first 446 terms from Jonathan Wellons, terms <= 10^27)
%H A136910 Jovan Radenkovic, <a href="https://qr.ae/pFD3HP">The OEIS sequence A136910 lists (...). What is the estimate of A136910(n) for extremely large n?</a>, Quora.com, Oct. 4, 2026
%H A136910 Jonathan Wellons, <a href="https://web.archive.org/web/20090206165028/http://jonathanwellons.com/shared-digits/">Tables of Shared Digits</a> [snapshot on web.archive.org, as of Feb. 2009].
%H A136910 <a href="/index/Sq#squares">Index to sequences related to squares</a>
%e A136910 505000500500005^2 = 255025505505255550255005000025.
%e A136910 a(n = 217, 302, 416, 442, 570, ...) = 5005020005005, 50050200050050, 500502000500500, 505000200000505, 5005020005005000, ... are the first terms with a digit 2. (Divided by 5, they have digits 0, 1, and 4.) - _M. F. Hasler_, Oct 13 2026
%t A136910 Select[FromDigits/@Tuples[{0,2,5},9],SubsetQ[{0,2,5},IntegerDigits[ #^2]]&] (* _Harvey P. Dale_, May 30 2019 *)
%o A136910 (PARI) apply( {A136910_row(n, d=[0,2,5])=my(r=List(), u=[10^(n-k)|k<-[1..n]]~); forvec(v=[[if(k>1,1,#d),#d]|k<-[1..n]], n=vecextract(d,v)*u; #setminus(Set(digits(n^2)), d)||listput(r,n)); Vec(r)}, [0..5]) \\ A136910_row(n) returns all terms with n digits, where 0 has 0 digits. - _M. F. Hasler_, Oct 12 2026
%o A136910 (Python)
%o A136910 def A136910(n, inc = False):
%o A136910     if inc: # return next larger candidate term
%o A136910         if n%1000 < 505: return n + max(5, n%100*9)
%o A136910         c = 3; n //= 1000
%o A136910         while (r := n%10) > 2: n //= 10; c += 1
%o A136910         return (n+3)*10**c if r else (n+2)*10**c+5 if n else 5*10**c
%o A136910     if not hasattr(A:=A136910, 'data'): A.data = [0,5]
%o A136910     while n>len(A.data):
%o A136910         k = A(A.data[-1], 1)
%o A136910         while not all(d in "025" for d in str(k**2)): k = A(k, 1)
%o A136910         A.data.append(k)
%o A136910     return A.data[n-1] # _M. F. Hasler_, Oct 12 2026
%Y A136910 Cf. A136808, A136810, ..., A137147 for other digit combinations.
